the day after a las vegas woman was  arrested and accused of starving and  neglecting her three horses the person  one who saved one is now sharing their  story it's been two years but she says  the animal is still recovering and she  hopes to help others catch similar  situations before they get this bad  sasha loftus live in studio with the  story you will only see on 8 news now  brian and denise the woman i spoke with  tells me her horse is still recovering  after all this time she said she wants  to see more action when it comes to all  animal cruelty cases  he's my best friend now a horse that's  been to hell and back is now living the  life he's always deserved  meet zeke you'd never know it now but he  used to look like this after his adopter  who didn't want to share her identity  saved him two years ago nothing was  taken care of he was still underweight  when he came home she says he was one of  three horses starved for months in the  northeast valley his hips were sticking  out like no other he was moving slow at  first  he didn't want to get up armalinda roca  was arrested this week accused of  keeping zeke and two other horses in  filthy conditions  with algae covered water buckets and no  food as far as i know he was pretty much  up to his belly and feces and  that's all he only had his feces to eat  it was a long difficult road his current  family wishes zeke never had to face  but with their love and daily dedication  he's now doing his best to embrace his  second chance at happiness he's still  not over anything  but he's getting there and he's getting  better by the day and slowly understands  he'll never have to no hunger fear or  pain ever again he knows he's going to  be fed he knows that it's not going to  stop again  zeke and the two other horses were  rescued in 2020 one of them later died  irmalinda roca faces three felony  charges for wilford on malicious torture  of an animal she was released on her own  recognizance and ordered not to have  contact with animals she's due back in  court on august 9. denise
 

